(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-human-nutrition-quiz"]={"title":"Human Nutrition — CAM/Nutrition Exam 1","category":"CAM / Nutrition","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which definition best matches the lecture's definition of \"nutrition\"?","choices":["The process of digesting and absorbing macronutrients.","The regulation of body temperature through diet.","The measurement of body fat percentage.","The science that links foods to health."],"answer":3,"exp":"Nutrition is defined in lecture as the science that links foods to health — food provides both energy (calories) and the compounds needed to build and maintain body cells. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 5 (Nutrition)"},{"q":"To be classified as an \"essential nutrient,\" a substance must meet which set of criteria per lecture?","choices":["It must provide at least 4 kcal per gram, be required in gram quantities daily, and be classified separately from micronutrients.","A specific biological function must be identified, omitting it from the diet causes a decline in function, and replacing it restores normal function.","It must be synthesized entirely by the body, never require dietary intake, and remain unaffected by omission from the diet.","It must appear on the Nutrition Facts label, list a Percent Daily Value, and meet FDA disclosure requirements for that nutrient."],"answer":1,"exp":"Per lecture, an essential nutrient must have one specific biological function identified, its omission from the diet must cause a decline in that function, and replacing it in the diet must restore normal function. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 5 (Nutrition — Essential Nutrient)"},{"q":"Which characteristic distinguishes minerals from vitamins per lecture?","choices":["Minerals are only needed by the body in the presence of dietary fat.","Minerals provide 4 kcal per gram.","Minerals are inorganic substances that do not contain carbon atoms.","Minerals are destroyed easily during cooking."],"answer":2,"exp":"Minerals are defined as inorganic substances that contain no carbon atoms; unlike some vitamins, they are not destroyed during cooking and, like vitamins, produce no calories/energy. — Human Nutrition lecture, slides 18–19 (Vitamins / Minerals)"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the six classes of nutrients presented in lecture?","choices":["Fiber","Lipids","Minerals","Water"],"answer":0,"exp":"The six classes of nutrients are carbohydrates, proteins, lipids, vitamins, minerals, and water. Fiber is a type of complex carbohydrate, not a separate nutrient class of its own. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 9 (6 Classes of Nutrients)"},{"q":"Which nutrient provides approximately 9 kcal per gram — more than double any other macronutrient?","choices":["Protein","Fat (lipid)","Alcohol","Carbohydrate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Fat/lipid provides about 9 kcal/gram, versus about 4 kcal/gram for carbohydrate and protein and about 7 kcal/gram for alcohol, making it the most energy-dense of the four. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 16 (Calorie Overview)"},{"q":"Carbohydrate and protein each provide approximately how many kilocalories per gram?","choices":["9 kcal/g","4 kcal/g","7 kcal/g","0 kcal/g"],"answer":1,"exp":"Both carbohydrate and protein provide about 4 kcal/gram — identical energy density, even though the body uses them very differently. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 16 (Calorie Overview)"},{"q":"A food's \"energy density\" is determined by comparing which two properties?","choices":["Its fiber content and its vitamin content.","Its macronutrient ratio and its price.","Its sugar content and its sodium content.","Its kilocalorie content and its weight."],"answer":3,"exp":"Energy density compares a food's calorie (kcal) content with its weight — foods like salsa or vegetable soup are low-energy-density because they pack few calories per gram, while foods like butter are high-energy-density. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 51 (Energy Density)"},{"q":"A sandwich contains 46 grams of carbohydrate, 14 grams of fat, 45 grams of protein, and 0 grams of alcohol. Using 4/9/4/7 kcal per gram, approximately how many total kilocalories does it provide?","choices":["550 kcal","360 kcal","490 kcal","405 kcal"],"answer":2,"exp":"184 kcal from carbohydrate (46 × 4) + 126 kcal from fat (14 × 9) + 180 kcal from protein (45 × 4) + 0 kcal from alcohol = 490 kcal total, matching the lecture's grilled chicken sandwich example. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 23 (Calculating Calories)"},{"q":"A patient weighs 220 pounds. Using the conversion that 1 kilogram equals about 2.2 pounds, approximately what is that weight in kilograms?","choices":["120 kg","110 kg","100 kg","90 kg"],"answer":2,"exp":"220 lb ÷ 2.2 = 100 kg, matching the lecture's instruction to divide pounds by 2.2 to convert to kilograms. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 26 (Metric System Summary)"},{"q":"Per the metric system summary in lecture, approximately how many grams are in one pound?","choices":["454 grams","1,000 grams","250 grams","500 grams"],"answer":0,"exp":"One pound equals about 454 grams, per the lecture's metric conversion summary. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 26 (Metric System Summary)"},{"q":"Per the metric conversions given in lecture, about how many milliliters are in one teaspoon?","choices":["240 mL","5 mL","30 mL","15 mL"],"answer":1,"exp":"One teaspoon is about 5 milliliters, per lecture's metric summary. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 27 (Metric System Summary)"},{"q":"Per the 2018 CDC statistics cited in lecture, approximately what percentage of the U.S. population was classified as overweight?","choices":["41%","15.8%","72.5%","26%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Lecture cites 2018 CDC data showing about 72.5% of the U.S. population classified as overweight, compared to 41% obese and 26% normal weight. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 30 (2018 CDC stats on obesity)"},{"q":"Per the BMI guidelines presented in lecture, a BMI of 32 falls into which category?","choices":["Healthy body weight","Class 1 Obesity","Class 2 Obesity","Overweight"],"answer":1,"exp":"Class 1 Obesity spans a BMI of 30–34.99, so a BMI of 32 falls squarely in that range. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 29 (Obesity Definition — BMI Guidelines)"},{"q":"Per lecture, how has increasing affluence in North America contributed to leading causes of death?","choices":["It has led to decreased alcohol consumption, reduced use of processed foods, and less reliance on convenience dining.","It has led to increased physical activity, reduced sugar intake, and more consistent structured exercise habits.","It has led to increased consumption of fiber, whole grains, and other minimally processed staple foods.","It has led to more sedentary lifestyles and higher intakes of animal fat, sugar, salt, and alcohol."],"answer":3,"exp":"Lecture notes that although North Americans live longer with more diverse food and lifestyle choices, affluence has also driven more sedentary lifestyles and higher intakes of animal fat, sugar, salt, and alcohol — factors that feed into leading causes of death. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 42 (Longer Healthier Lives)"},{"q":"Per the 2018 NHANES data cited in lecture, approximately what percentage of American kilocalorie intake came from carbohydrates?","choices":["3%","35.8%","46.7%","15.8%"],"answer":2,"exp":"NHANES 2018 data cited in lecture shows about 46.7% of kcal from carbohydrate, which falls within the Food and Nutrition Board's advised 45%–65% range. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 32 (Assessing the Current North American Eating Pattern)"},{"q":"According to the 2018 NHANES data cited in lecture, which macronutrient's share of kilocalorie intake (35.8%) slightly exceeded the top end of the Food and Nutrition Board's advised range?","choices":["Fat","Carbohydrate","Protein","Alcohol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Fat accounted for 35.8% of kcal intake against an advised range of 20%–35%, putting the North American average slightly above the top of the recommended range. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 32 (Assessing the Current North American Eating Pattern)"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed in lecture as a recommended improvement to the North American diet?","choices":["Increase intake of solid fats over plant oils.","Increase sodium and added sugar intake.","Decrease the variety and quantity of fruits and vegetables consumed.","Choose plant sources of protein rather than animal proteins."],"answer":3,"exp":"Lecture lists choosing plant protein sources over animal proteins, along with choosing plant oils over solid fats, moderating sugar/salt/alcohol, and increasing fruit and vegetable variety, as needed improvements to the North American diet. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 33 (Assessing the Current North American Diet)"},{"q":"Per the lecture's list of food-choice influences, which is cited as the single biggest driver of food choice (\"#1\")?","choices":["Flavor, texture, and appearance","Marketing and advertising","Overall cost and economics","General nutrition content"],"answer":0,"exp":"Lecture explicitly ranks flavor, texture, and appearance as the #1 influence on food choice, ahead of early influences, habits/availability/convenience, marketing, restaurant dining, time, cost, sustainability, and nutrition. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 3 (Food Choice Influences)"},{"q":"Which of the following is explicitly listed among the lecture's food-choice influences?","choices":["Early influences","Employer wellness policy","Genetic testing results","Local weather patterns"],"answer":0,"exp":"\"Early influences\" (paralleling early childhood experiences shaping eating habits) is explicitly listed among the lecture's food-choice influences, alongside flavor/texture/appearance, habits/availability, marketing, dining out, time, cost, sustainability, and nutrition. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 3 (Food Choice Influences)"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT listed among the lecture's food-choice influences?","choices":["Marketing and advertising","Time and convenience","Cost and economics","Genetic testing results"],"answer":3,"exp":"Genetic testing results do not appear on lecture's list of food-choice influences — the list covers flavor/texture/appearance, early influences, habits/availability/convenience, marketing/advertising, restaurant dining, time/convenience, cost/economics, sustainability, and nutrition. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 3 (Food Choice Influences)"},{"q":"Per the lecture, which set correctly names the three functional categories of nutrients?","choices":["Provide energy; promote growth, development, and maintenance; and regulate body processes","Provide flavor, texture, and appearance","Digest, absorb, and excrete waste","Build muscle, store fat, and produce heat only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the lecture groups nutrient functions into providing energy, promoting growth/development/maintenance, and regulating body processes. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 8"},{"q":"According to the lecture, how are vitamins classified, and which type does cooking destroy more readily?","choices":["Organic and inorganic; cooking destroys inorganic vitamins more readily","Fat-soluble (A, D, E, K) and water-soluble (B vitamins and C); cooking destroys water-soluble vitamins more readily","Major and trace; cooking destroys trace vitamins more readily","Simple and complex; cooking destroys complex vitamins more readily"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: vitamins are fat-soluble (A, D, E, K) or water-soluble (B and C), and cooking destroys water-soluble ones more readily. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 18"},{"q":"How does the lecture define minerals?","choices":["Compounds easily destroyed by heat during cooking","The body's main structural building material","Inorganic substances that contain no carbon atoms, are not destroyed by cooking, and provide no calories","Organic compounds that provide 4 kilocalories per gram"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: minerals are inorganic (no carbon), are not destroyed during cooking, and produce no calories. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 19"},{"q":"Which set of roles for water does the lecture describe?","choices":["Provides about 9 kilocalories per gram of energy","Serves as the body's main energy store","Is destroyed during cooking and must be replaced","Acts as a solvent and lubricant, transports nutrients and waste, and regulates body temperature"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: water serves as a solvent and lubricant, transports nutrients and waste, and regulates body temperature. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 20"},{"q":"Per the body mass index guidelines in the lecture, a value of 37 falls into which category?","choices":["Class 2 obesity","Class 1 obesity","Overweight","Class 3 obesity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Class 2 obesity spans a body mass index of 35–39.99, so 37 falls in that range. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 29"},{"q":"According to the lecture, how often are the Dietary Guidelines for Americans published, and by whom?","choices":["Only once, with no scheduled updates","Every 5 years, by the United States Department of Agriculture and the Department of Health and Human Services","Every year, by the Food and Drug Administration","Every 10 years, by the World Health Organization"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the Dietary Guidelines for Americans are published every 5 years by the Department of Agriculture and the Department of Health and Human Services. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 35"},{"q":"The lecture describes Healthy People 2030 as a set of national health objectives published on what schedule?","choices":["Every year","Every 2 years","Every 10 years","Every 5 years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Healthy People objectives are published every 10 years. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 35"},{"q":"How does the lecture define a functional food, and what example does it give?","choices":["A food engineered to contain no calories","A prepackaged meal replacement shake","Any food eaten primarily for its flavor","A food providing health benefits beyond its traditional nutrients, such as tomatoes containing lycopene"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: functional foods provide benefits beyond traditional nutrients; the lecture cites tomatoes' phytochemical lycopene. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 45"},{"q":"How does the MyPlate icon in the lecture divide the plate among food groups?","choices":["Fruits and vegetables cover half; grains about one-fourth; protein the remaining one-fourth; with a cup of dairy alongside","Protein covers half the plate, with grains filling the rest","The plate is divided equally into four food groups with no dairy","Grains cover half the plate, with vegetables as a side"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: MyPlate shows fruits and vegetables as half the plate, grains and protein each about one-fourth, plus a cup of dairy. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 57"},{"q":"Which limits does the lecture's healthy eating pattern set for saturated fat, added sugars, and sodium?","choices":["Sodium under 500 milligrams and no fat limit","Saturated fat under 10% of calories, added sugars under 10% of calories, and sodium under 2,300 milligrams","Saturated fat under 50% of calories and no sodium limit","No limit on added sugars as long as calories are met"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the pattern limits saturated fat and added sugars to less than 10% of calories each and sodium to less than 2,300 milligrams. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 56"}]};
